# actions AGENTS.md
CI verifies.
x runs every check CI runs; push the branch and read the result on the pull request.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
The workflow resolves no visibility, provider or machine size itself.
Public callers carry only their own public configuration.
Choices at run time follow only the event or the changed files, such as read-only cache safety for pull requests.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
Each step has its own time limit, and the job has none, so the job ends within their sum.
The mbx step's 7 minutes apply to the restore and to the export separately.
A failed or timed-out export leaves the job green.
The export removes the Cargo target first, after every step that reads it.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
A ruleset requires only x, as x from a caller job named x.
So the jobs behind it can change without a ruleset change.
An upstream failure cannot skip a required check into green.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
The mise cache key carries x and the hash of every x.
So the build job's cache holds the profile's tools, and no other job restores it.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
x fails when a required job failed or was cancelled.
It passes a cancelled run only when a newer run replaced it, for the same event, branch and head commit.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
A pull request, or a run on another branch, reads the volume and never commits it.

# actions docs/secrets.md
The install sets x, so mise takes the download URL and checksum from the caller's x and writes no lock entries.
A gitleaks entry missing for the platform of the x input fails the job instead of slowing it.
The default runner is linux-x64; a caller that picks a runner of another platform runs x for it.
x skips the caller's x, such as x.

# actions docs/secrets.md
A scheduled or dispatched run scans the whole history.
The pre-push hook scans only the commits it pushes, so the full scan runs here once a day.

# actions docs/secrets.md
The job installs gitleaks from the caller's x, with x.
x lets the mise action download it through the release mirror.

# actions macos-signing/README.md
x loads the CI Developer ID identities and the notary key for a macOS release job.
x removes them again.

# actions mise/README.md
The action requests the job's OIDC token for the mirror's address.
It exchanges that token at the mirror's x for a session token that lasts six hours.
No caller holds a mirror secret.

# actions policy/README.md
Each entry names the rule, the workflow file and a reason.
An entry with no reason excepts nothing.

# actions tasks/README.md
A rustdoc warning fails x before docs.rs sees it.
It builds every feature, even when a crate's docs.rs metadata lists fewer.
So it checks every gated item docs.rs could render.

# actions tasks/README.md
That table chooses what docs.rs builds: x, or a x list.
A list fits when all features would build heavy dependencies that gate no documented item.

# actions tasks/README.md
No tracked Rust, TOML or shell file names x or x.
Neither does any file in a mise file-task directory.
A crate names those lints only to change their level, which the standard forbids.

# bun-release AGENTS.md
This is standing procedure, not one-shot migration: every new npm name hits the same wall.
Re-running it is a guard — existing versions skip publish, matching trust is left alone, a mismatch fails.
Delete the helper when npm ships first-publish OIDC (npm/cli#8544).
The consumer mise task maps the same staged packages CI will publish; do not hand-type a package list or add a bun-release CLI.

# bun-release AGENTS.md
Versioning is changeset-driven — CI owns the bump, publish, tag, and GitHub Release.
Registry is public npm with OIDC trusted publishing.
Runners are GitHub-hosted x.
A GitHub Release is changelog notes plus the x tag.

# ctl-core AGENTS.md
A dated nightly carries only rustfmt: x runs on it for x's unstable options, and x builds on it as docs.rs does.
The mise pin, x, and x move together in one commit; x fails when they differ.
CI uses the same pins and caches through x.

# ctl-core AGENTS.md
x pins stable 1.99.1 for build, lint, and test, with x.
That routes Cargo through mr-boxington so worktrees share one build store.

# ctl-core AGENTS.md
Measured on Linux and macOS with doc-tests included, cargo-nextest was 1.05 to 14 times slower (it starts one process per test).
mbx test scheduling or a x cap slowed concurrent runs.
Do not reintroduce them without new measurements.

# ctl-core AGENTS.md
Local edits survive an update.
x allows the README banner's HTML, and x ignores commit hashes cited in prose.
Change a shared rule in the template, then take its new tag here.

# ctl-core AGENTS.md
Markdown prose uses semantic line breaks.
Each sentence starts a new line, and a long sentence may also break at a clause.
No line is wrapped at a fixed column, so x turns off the line-length rule.

# ctl-core ctl-core/AGENTS.md
The crate-root x sections in x are the docs.rs landing page, and x there is authoritative for the feature graph.
The README is the GitHub page and does not copy them.
Crate-root re-exports stay x, so the page lists modules once; the prelude documents the flat surface.

# ctl-core ctl-core/AGENTS.md
Traits are the exception, with every type a required trait method takes or returns.
cargo-semver-checks 0.51.0 treats a trait as public-API-sealed (x) when any x path reaches it or such a type.
This holds even when another path is public.
So x, x, and x (returned by x) stay visible at the root.

# ctl-core ctl-core/AGENTS.md
The automatic terminal-width buffer defaults to two columns and reads x; the automatic minimum defaults to 20.
When no width is detected, output lays out to a fallback width of 80, and x disables it.
x, x, and x expose the buffer, ordered environment names, minimum, and fallback.
x returns its settings as a x from x.

# ctl-core ctl-core/AGENTS.md
A setter on a builder type takes the name of its setting (x, x, x), never a x prefix.
A reader of the same setting gets another name, such as x, or no method at all.
A method that reads the environment starts with x, so a caller sees which methods are not pure.

# ctl-core ctl-core/AGENTS.md
Write a new lock with the pinned mise, 2026.10.25.
A newer mise writes a new file as lockfile version 4.
CI's mise cannot read that version.

# ctl-core ctl-core/AGENTS.md
The format is chosen, never guessed.
x reads it from the extension (x, x, x, any case).
It refuses every other extension before reading, and names the feature a known extension needs.

# ctl-core ctl-core/tests/parity/DIFFERENCES.md
Every way a file in x differs from its file of the same name in x, grouped by kind.
x is the comfy-table renderer of 0.6.x and stays frozen; x is the ctl-layout renderer.
31 of the 208 files differ.
Each kind names one example as case, width, and mode, and the design decision of the x change that produces it.
No kind is an unfixable regression.

# ctl-core ctl-term/AGENTS.md
x: a real child process piped to x.
It needs the x feature, and on a target that is not Unix it prints a skip line.
It runs without libtest, because libtest prints its own banner to stdout, and x quits after the first line.
The next libtest write would then fail before the code under test ran.
Keep that target at x in x.

# ctl-core ctl-text/README.md
x lays text out as plain data before anything is written to a terminal.
A x is text in one style, and a x is a list of spans.
A x is a list of lines that wraps to a width.

# ctl-core docs/brand.md
A 32-unit square, corner radius 6.
Two discs of r 8, centred x and x.
The centres sit on x, 7 units apart on each axis.
The figure is symmetric about the square's diagonal and clears the field by 4.5 units on every side.

# ctl-core docs/brand.md
Set in Geist Mono (OFL) and converted to outlines, so nothing depends on a font at render time.
Sizes: wordmark Black 60px with −3 tracking, tagline Medium 17px, chip Regular 16px.
To change the wording, reshape with x + x at those sizes.
Do not add a x element.

# ctl-core docs/presentation.md
Sometimes neither a terminal nor x gives a width.
Agent and CI captures, where every stream is piped, are examples.
Then ctl-core lays out to a fallback width of 80.
x changes it and x renders tables at their natural width.
The fallback is separate from the minimum, which applies only to a detected width.

# ctl-core docs/presentation.md
The consumer's setter wins over the variable.
x, x, x, and x replace each ordered name list, and an empty slice disables lookup.
A value outside the list is ignored, and x prints one warning line that lists the accepted values.

# ctl-core openspec/specs/declared-input/spec.md
A refusal caused by a limit SHALL name the file, the line when known, the limit's field and value, and x.
A limit hit while reading x itself SHALL point at the tool's process default instead.
Without x, the message SHALL end with x.

# exa-cli README.md
x skips the read and overwrites the entry.
Use only when staleness provably matters.

# exa-cli skills/exa/SKILL.md
Raw body is JSON text for that command's generated Hey API schema.
Optique parses it; do not pass a file path:

# fanctl AGENTS.md
No daemon, no resume hook.
The chip runs the curves itself.
The driver (x, x) writes back after S3 resume the curves written through its attributes since it loaded, so a boot-time x covers resume too.

# fanctl AGENTS.md
Write order follows the driver.
The driver checks each point write alone, against the curve as it stands, and refuses one that leaves the curve falling.
fanctl orders the writes so every intermediate curve stays non-decreasing.
The rule, with its proof, is on x; a test runs it over every pair of non-decreasing sequences of 7 values from 0 to 4.
Every point is written, even one that holds its value, because the driver writes back after resume only the curves written since it loaded.

# fanctl AGENTS.md
Duty in percent, one decimal.
The config states duty in percent with at most one decimal; it maps to the driver's 0-255 by rounding to the nearest step.
One decimal names every step, so x prints a percent that maps back to the same step, which may differ from the percent the config gave (49.6 and 49.4 are both step 126).

# fanctl AGENTS.md
Headers, not channels.
A config names fans by board header (x, x, x …).
A table per board maps each header to its pwm channel; a header the table does not list is an error.

# fanctl AGENTS.md
Validate before writing.
A config whose temperatures or duties fall from one point to the next, or that is out of range, is refused before anything is written.

# fanctl README.md
Each fan takes exactly 7 points: x in whole degrees C (0 to 127) and x in percent (0 to 100, one decimal at most).
Neither may fall from one point to the next.
A header the file does not name keeps its curve.

# fanctl src/instructions.md
x writes declared fan curves into the Nuvoton NCT6687D-R of an MSI board through the x hwmon driver, on Linux.
The chip runs the curves itself; fanctl leaves no process behind.
It finds the driver's device as the one x whose x reads x; the number is never fixed.

# fanctl src/instructions.md
x maps a board header to its curve.
On the MS-7E70 the headers are x (pwm1), x (pwm2) and x to x (pwm3 to pwm8).
A header the board does not have is refused.
A header the file does not name keeps the curve it has.

# fanctl src/instructions.md
x prints the curves the driver holds now, as a config that x takes back unchanged.
A duty prints with at most one decimal, which maps to the same 0-255 step.
A header the driver exposes no curve for is left out.

# fnox-export AGENTS.md
The suite MUST stay hermetic and re-runnable.
x sandboxes x and the x dirs and sets x so real fnox daemons (whose sockets live in x, outside the sandbox) are never spawned and cannot leak across runs.
A test that needs the daemon flag stubs x (see x) — never force a real daemon in the suite.

# fnox-export README.md
A selector exports matching source keys unchanged.
Supports x wildcards.
Bare x exports all profile keys and succeeds even when the profile is empty.

# fnox-export README.md
The plugin sets x on every fnox process it spawns, and spawns nothing when it runs with that variable set.
This matters when x on x is a mise shim: each plugin call to fnox is then mise again, which evaluates the env and runs the plugin again.
Without the guard that recursion fills the per-user process table.
With it, the nested evaluation returns an empty, uncacheable result and the shim runs the real fnox.

# forkctl AGENTS.md
CI sets explicit Cargo and rustup homes beneath mise's data directory, so mise provisions its own rustup and caches the actual toolchains instead of reusing runner-installed proxies.
Both stable and nightly use minimal profiles with explicit components.
The macOS gate verifies the provider and compiler paths; a manual dispatch skips the install cache to prove cold provisioning before running the normal gates.

# forkctl docs/brand.md
The square mark has one rust shape.
The banner repeats that rust on the chip so the header matches the mark.
It is the same accent, not a second role.

# forkctl examples/README.md
Merge the relevant sections from x into the consumer.
The immutable remote catalog exposes one mounted x task whose Usage grammar comes directly from forkctl's Clap tree:

# forkctl openspec/specs/manifest-upgrade/spec.md
On a schema 2 manifest with uncommitted changes, x SHALL fail with x, because an upgrade whose bookkeeping refresh failed leaves the rewritten files uncommitted.
When the bookkeeping refresh fails, the error SHALL name the commands that finish the commit.
When writing the rewritten files fails, the error SHALL name the x that undoes them.

# forkctl README.md
Every long option also reads x, such as x or x.
A flag on the command line wins over its variable, and an empty variable counts as unset.
Help shows each variable beside its flag.

# forkctl README.md
A scheduled x job needs a GitHub App installation token, not the workflow's x.
A rebase replays upstream changes to x, and x can never be granted x.
A pull request opened with x also starts no workflows, so the proposal would get no CI.

# forkctl README.md
The extension selects the codec: x/x reads and rewrites canonical YAML; x reads and rewrites canonical JSON.
Both deserialize into the same typed x, execute the same handlers, and produce the same generated evidence.
Unknown or missing extensions are rejected — forkctl never sniffs bytes or silently changes a file's format.

# forkctl README.md
All three transitions require a clean fully checked stack and no active patch.
They create an annotated recovery tag and publication operation, mutate the series through StGit, regenerate the manifest/ledger/exports, and stop at the same exact-lease atomic x gate as rebase.
The bookkeeping patch cannot be removed or disabled.

# forkctl README.md
Every patch commit carries matching x, x, and x trailers.
Source patches precede tooling patches.
Every source export is generated deterministically as x; tooling patches have no export.
The final tooling patch owns manifest, ledger, exports, and integration files.

# forkctl README.md
Disabled patches are absent from the source tree and active StGit series.
Their metadata, former commit, original position, reason, and recovery evidence remain under x; fresh clone hydration fetches those recovery tags.
Enabling imports the preserved patch at its former position with a 3-way apply and can continue or abort through the normal operation journal on conflict.
Permanent removal writes the same evidence to manifest history instead.

# forkctl README.md
Clap remains the command grammar.
Templates under x retain skill and instruction prose while ctl-core x renders their shared mounted invocation/no-x rule and visible command inventory.
Tests byte-compare both committed documents.

# forkctl README.md
If the branch already matches while a ready operation remains, x verifies every recorded recovery ref before clearing the local journal and snapshot.
Exact refs make the retry idempotent; missing required evidence is published atomically under the unchanged branch lease, while mismatched evidence fails closed.

# forkctl skills/forkctl/SKILL.md
x pushes x under a lease and opens or updates its draft pull request with x; a repeat updates that branch and pull request in place.
Do not merge a proposal pull request: from a clone whose x is the reviewed candidate, run x.
It refuses unless the proposal tree matches x.
x names a branch, tag, or URL other than x.

# forkctl skills/forkctl/SKILL.md
JSON is never coloured, whatever x or x say, and a parse error under x is a JSON error even when the bad argument comes first.
Keep stdout as exactly one JSON document and treat schema-derived command names, arguments, results, notices, error codes, and details as authoritative.
Do not parse pretty tables or error prose.

# forkctl skills/forkctl/SKILL.md
Each transition requires a clean checked stack, creates immutable recovery evidence, regenerates exports and the ledger, and stops at the ordinary atomic exact-lease publication gate.
A disabled patch is absent from the source tree but retained in manifest x with its former commit and recovery tag.
x / x own conflicts during deletion, replay, or re-enable.
The bookkeeping patch can never be removed or disabled.

# forkctl skills/forkctl/SKILL.md
Pretty output is for operators.
Automation uses JSON output or the local one-request API:

# forkctl src/instructions.md
Checks never stage or rewrite.
StGit refresh invokes the consumer's pre-commit hook and consumes its final index.
A hook that leaves files dirty, such as a regenerated lockfile, fails the final check after the commit is written.
A refresh or edit then stays an operation in phase x: stage the paths the patch owns, such as a x the new pin rewrote, restore or remove the others, and run x; or run x, which discards them.
A remove, disable, enable, or rebase stays in x: restore those paths and run x, which checks again, or abort.
x refuses while any operation is in flight.

# forkctl src/instructions.md
Everyday follow-up is a new top patch.
x on a non-top patch is refused unless you pass x.
That flag unapplies everything above the patch (including tooling that owns mise).
It is recoverable via x / x; it is not the everyday edit path.

# forkctl src/instructions.md
forkctl snapshots x and x into x at operation start.
If they vanish from the worktree, it restores them as temporary files so x still works.
Continue/abort remove those temps before StGit reapplies later patches, so they cannot block x.
The tracked manifest is not copied into the worktree; the Git-private JSON snapshot already covers operation continue/abort.

# forkctl src/instructions.md
Forkctl exposes commands; it does not own a hook manager:

# hakkiri AGENTS.md
Release scope.
Release 1 is the CLI: x and x, run from a mise task, CI and a pre-commit hook.
Release 2 is the language server, on the same per-file engine.

# hakkiri AGENTS.md
Structure first.
A rule selects text by its scope in the document tree; a pattern only narrows that selection.
A rule that needs a regular expression to find whether text sits in code, a link or a comment shows a missing scope.

# hakkiri AGENTS.md
Language profiles.
Each source language has a profile that maps its comments and doc comments into the tree.
Every comment reads as Markdown, plain x comments included; TSDoc and JSDoc keep their tags.
String literals are never prose.
The first release ships Rust, TypeScript, JavaScript, Markdown and MDX.

# hakkiri openspec/specs/document-tree/spec.md
In a x comment of a TypeScript or JavaScript file, a tag name SHALL carry no prose scope.
A type in braces after a tag SHALL carry none either, and neither SHALL the name after x, x, x, x, x, x, x or x.
The description after them SHALL be prose, and the body of x SHALL be code.
A line inside a fenced block SHALL begin no tag.

# hakkiri openspec/specs/document-tree/spec.md
Adjacent line comments of the same type SHALL join into one comment node, read as one Markdown text.

# hakkiri openspec/specs/node-segments/spec.md
Every node of the document tree reads its text from segments: byte ranges of the file that hold prose and no syntax.
A check reads the text, and an alert points at the span that covers it.

# hakkiri openspec/specs/node-segments/spec.md
THEN the x node has the segments of its two cells, and its text is x

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
The live test owns temporary homes, registries, sockets and fake agents.
It proves real Ctrl-click dispatch, native resume arguments, double-click rejection, wrong-pane/stale-link rejection, output-only startup, and stop/crash recovery.
It never touches an existing user session.
Set x to test a copy of a full config; test-only shell/update/history overrides affect only the copy.
The binary must be built before running live tests.

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
Herdr runs the startup hook after restoring a stopped server and opening its API.
This covers both x → x and crash recovery.
The plugin also prints a notice when a live agent exits and the native session is still retained (x with x).
That is x and process death, not Pi x and not x.
Merely attaching to an already running server, linking/enabling the plugin, or reloading configuration does not rerun startup.
Do not restart a busy server to preview it; the next normal restore will use the installed plugin.

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
The action prints one JSON document to the plugin log, which x returns.
Each paused pane names its directory and agent kind, which a JSON API Resume request names too, and the launch flags its notice shows.
x appears only for a named Pi session, and x is null when no launch was recorded:

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
OSC links are enabled by default.
Herdr preserves them; guessing outer-terminal capabilities from a server's environment is unreliable, especially before any client attaches.
Use an explicit setting instead:

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
Before launching, the plugin verifies the clicked pane, native session, directory, shell PID/start time and PTY.
Busy panes, missing transcripts, stale history links and reused tickets are rejected.
An exclusive claim prevents two clicks from starting two agents.
A readiness timeout does not trigger a retry: an agent may already have started, so inspect the pane and plugin log.

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
This prints the literal, copyable native resume command instead of clickable links.
Set x, or remove the file, to restore links.
Only this optional plugin-owned file is parsed; Herdr's config is never parsed by the plugin.
Settings take effect on the next startup hook.

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
A small Herdr plugin that writes a colored, compact notice into restored shell scrollback.
Nothing starts until you click Resume.
No prefilled commands, prompt manipulation, background daemon or browser callback server.

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
The restored pane already has Herdr's native environment and cwd: x, x, current Herdr socket/binary and workspace/tab/pane IDs; inherited x and x are removed.
The command inherits the restored shell's environment.
Herdr does not reconstruct original flags, model settings or custom launch variables.
This plugin applies flags only when a caller handed them over (see Launch flags) and never invents them; launch variables are not restored.
If cwd changed, the click is rejected rather than silently changing directories.

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
Resume: Ctrl-click, including on macOS.
Herdr handles the custom OSC 8 URI locally through the plugin's declared link handler.
It starts the retained conversation in that same pane through x.
The live routing name is an opaque ticket hash so it stays unique; the sidebar x token uses x (the Pi session name when present, otherwise x).

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
File-link opening depends on your outer terminal and OS.
Remote file paths are not translated to local paths.
No OS custom-protocol registration is required for Resume.
The link contains an opaque ticket, not a shell command.

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
An explicit Pi session name appears when its retained transcript contains x metadata.
Unnamed sessions show just x; no title is inferred from messages, IDs or filenames.
The latest name wins, including an empty name that clears it.
Names are capped at 64 characters; unsafe or unavailable names are omitted without preventing resume.
Other agents retain the same compact x heading.

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
Retained sessions leave the agents sidebar when the process exits: Herdr has no paused or hibernated agent state.
List them with:

# herdr-restore-notice README.md
A client of Herdr's JSON API, such as a phone client that asked its user first, can request the same resume without a click.
It invokes the x action with the pane and the directory and agent kind it showed:

# opencode-cursor-inference AGENTS.md
x is the only permitted private build dependency.
Import exact helpers, bundle and tree-shake them, keep GitHub Packages credentials distinct from release App credentials, and prove private names/source paths are absent from every runtime artifact.

# opencode-cursor-inference AGENTS.md
Never read an installed Cursor IDE or its storage to derive identity.

# opencode-cursor-inference AGENTS.md
x opens or updates the Version Packages pull request using the x GitHub App.

# opencode-cursor-inference docs/decisions.md
Decision: This supersedes the preceding pre-release hold.
x is the public bootstrap release.
Changesets, npm OIDC trusted publishing, and the Version Packages workflow own subsequent versions, tags, and GitHub Releases.

# opencode-cursor-inference docs/decisions.md
Consequences: Do not generate a new conversation id per call.
Invocation ids remain unique per provider call.
Route-key changes finish the old outer run, retire its HTTP/2 connection completely, and only then create the replacement run; Cursor can retire that connection after x, so immediate reuse or replacement before close can abort the first request after a model switch.

# opencode-cursor-inference docs/decisions.md
Decision: Cache files represent only catalogs produced by a successful authenticated refresh.
A stale but structurally valid authenticated snapshot remains selectable after logout and after network or persistence failures.
Without a credential and without such a snapshot, expose exactly one real Cursor routing row: model id x, display name x, and wire model id x.
Never persist that fallback.

# opencode-cursor-inference README.md
Select Cursor and complete browser sign-in.
OpenCode stores the OAuth credential through its own auth contract.
This package does not maintain a credential database or secret store.

# opencode-cursor-inference README.md
Cursor supplies model inference and routing.
OpenCode remains the agent host: it owns the complete message history, arbitrary tool schemas, tool execution, continuation, sessions, branching, compaction, and transcript.

# opencode-cursor-inference README.md
Derives Cursor's machine identity from the host.
A random UUID is persisted in OpenCode's cache directory only when host derivation fails.

# opencode-cursor-inference README.md
The provider does not execute Cursor-native tools, project MCP servers, shell commands, or an agent loop.
Unsupported request controls and response capabilities fail closed.

# pi-auto-rename README.md
Sessions are named automatically after the first assistant response.
The extension uses the first user message to generate a 2-6 word Title Case name.
Use x (no args) at any point to regenerate the name from the full conversation history.

# pi-cursor AGENTS.md
Keep evidence classes separate: pinned IDE source, application-message captures, Pi 0.84.4 tag.
No packet-level claims.
No credential, machine id, account id, or billing value in fixtures.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
Run the script in Regenerating the outlines with this x instead.
Wordmark Black 104px with −5.2 tracking (the banner's −0.05 em), tagline Medium 24px, chip label Regular 20px; the three strings are the banner's, unchanged.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
Banner-only tints: x (muted on paper), x (muted on graphite), x / x (hairline).
The muted tone carries the tagline and the chip outline; the hairline carries nothing but the 1.5-unit inset border.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
Before changing the accent hue, check it against this pair: any replacement must have WCAG relative luminance in L ∈ [0.119, 0.249] to clear 3:1 against both x and x.
Magenta sits at 0.141.
A hue outside that band fails one field.
The band is specific to this field pair and does not transfer if the neutrals change.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
Slab thickness is 6.5 units, not 3.
At 3 units the same four-slab arrangement scatters into unrelated dashes at 16px and the rotation stops reading.
This was measured at 16px, not assumed, and it is the reason the slabs are slabs.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
pi.dev renders package media in a 232×129.625 box with x, so the source must be exactly 16:9 or the card crops it.
x and x are 3840×2160 (4K), with the SVG retaining a x design grid so the construction coordinates below stay readable.
Resolution is not the layout contract: 16:9 is.
Never reduce the raster below 4K.
The bordered card's inner image box still trims about 2.26 design-grid units off the top and bottom, so nothing may sit at the vertical edges.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
Inspect 1280×640 and smaller 2:1 downscales from the PNG, plus a centered 1.91:1 crop.
The mark's gaps and magenta slab must remain visible.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
That rotation is the construction rule.
Verify any edit by rotating one slab onto the next, never by eye.
The figure spans x on both axes, so it is centred on the square and clears the field by 4.5 units on every side.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
The mark slabs are baked at x, exactly as the banner bakes them at 3.5×.
The file carries no x attribute anywhere; a x wrapper makes every number in the table above unverifiable.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
Text clears 4.5:1 on both fields; the accent clears 3:1 for non-text on both.
That is what lets one magenta value serve light and dark without a second file.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
Every gap is 3 units — one at each corner of the whirl: between the top slab's right end at x and the right slab at x, and its three rotations.
That is 1.5px at 16px, which holds.
Closing any gap turns the pinwheel into a closed ring, which is a different and much weaker mark.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
Set in Geist Mono (OFL) 1.700 and converted to outlines: wordmark Black 60px with −3 tracking, tagline Medium 17px, chip Regular 16px.
Geist Mono advances 600/1000 units per glyph at every weight and applies no kerning, so a run of x characters at size x occupies x plus tracking — that is where the pen-end numbers in Banner geometry come from.

