# prose-style AGENTS.md
Books lag web and software usage.
When every choice is near zero, as with "wall of prose" and "wall of text", the counts decide nothing and the writer's wording stands.
The JSON form returns the same counts: x.

# prose-style AGENTS.md
Use word pairs that occur in ordinary English.
Check a doubtful pair, such as "plain fix", against its alternatives in Google Books Ngram.
Rewrite a pair near zero when an alternative scores well: "plain fix" has 0 per billion words, "quick fix" 280.

# prose-style AGENTS.md
x: the package configuration a project reads before its own.

# prose-style openspec/changes/bareurl-single-scan/proposal.md
Rust comments: Vale 3.26.0 reads only line comments as Markdown, so the x scope misses x blocks.
Check them with a Tengo script rule that scans the file once, or keep raw scope for block comments only.

# prose-style openspec/changes/bareurl-single-scan/proposal.md
Markdown: replace BareURL's raw-scope regex with a rule on the x scope.
Vale renders Markdown with goldmark and GFM, which turns a bare URL into a link, and maps x to x.
A bare URL or an autolink then shows as a link whose text is its URL, and fenced code and code spans never reach that scope.

# prose-style openspec/changes/frequency-habit-rules/proposal.md
The package's rules come from habits readers noticed, and nothing measures how much more often agents use them than people do.
research.md counts both sides: agent docs use semicolons 7.3 times as often as pre-2022 design docs, colons inside a sentence 4.0 times, and ", not the" contrasts 7.7 times.
A linear model that sees no topic words tells agent sentences from human ones with a held-out ROC AUC of 0.958 to 0.987 on sources it never trained on, so the habits are real and measurable.

# prose-style openspec/changes/frequency-habit-rules/research.md
Split sentences, then keep a sentence only when it has 5 to 60 words, starts with a capital or a quote, and holds no more than one stray symbol.
At least 85% of its tokens must look like words, and at least 70% of its words must be common English: a wordfreq 3.1.1 Zipf score of 3 or more.

# prose-style openspec/changes/frequency-habit-rules/research.md
Parse each format with its own parser: markdown-it-py 4.0.0 for Markdown, docutils 0.21.2 for reStructuredText, and Python's x for Stack Exchange HTML.
Only paragraphs and list items remain; code, tables, block quotes, headings and HTML blocks are dropped.

# prose-style openspec/changes/frequency-habit-rules/research.md
The spread filter kept 1,724 single words, 2,041 pairs and 453 triples, across 58 large agent sources and 12 human ones.
The verbs, adjectives and adverbs with the highest ratios fall into two groups.

# prose-style openspec/changes/frequency-habit-rules/research.md
Each candidate below scored high on both sides of the evidence: a large ratio and a wide spread.

# qctl AGENTS.md
The pull request's CI runs x; no hook runs it.
Lefthook: pre-commit checks formatting and scans the staged diff for credentials, commit-msg lints the message with committed (x), and pre-push runs x, the commit lint over every pushed commit, and a full-history credential scan.
Every hook runs with x.
A fresh clone arms them with x, through x's postinstall hook; CI never loads x, so no runner gets them.

# qctl AGENTS.md
Growing domain features use nested module trees, not flat helper files.
x is the row-edit entry point; its field, list, policy, and position mechanics live under x.
Document rendering helpers live under x, and dependency ordering under x.
Cross-feature APIs use the narrowest visibility that works.

# qctl docs/brand.md
A 32-unit square, corner radius 6, mirrored about x and inset 4.5 units left and right, 5 top and bottom.
The rule is 3 units wide at x, running x to x.
Two queued cells, 8×8, sit at x on x and x — their midpoint is the rule's centre, so the pair reads as balanced against it.
The task in flight is 8×10 at x, centred on the same line and taller than either queued cell.
Both side gaps to the rule are 2 units; closing them collapses the split, which is the whole statement.

