1.2 Teach x to rename each horizon x key in place when it upgrades schema 3, 4, or 5, and make every other verb refuse those versions with a message that names x.
Verify x, x, and x pass in CI.

# qctl openspec/specs/environment/spec.md
Each long option of every verb SHALL read x, with the long name upper-cased and x written as x.
A flag on the command line SHALL win over its variable, and an empty variable SHALL count as unset.
Help SHALL print x beside each bound option.

# qctl openspec/specs/ledger-style/spec.md
With x, x SHALL leave exactly one blank line between each pair of consecutive rows in the queue, the archive, and the horizon.
It SHALL NOT put a blank line between a list's key and its first row, and SHALL leave the space after a list's last row as written.
The blank line SHALL go above the comments a row owns.
x SHALL name the missing separator and write nothing.

# qctl openspec/specs/ledger-style/spec.md
The x block SHALL accept x, with the values x and x, which name yamled's one-line and automatic layouts.
A ledger that omits it SHALL behave as x.
Every writer and x SHALL lay out list items as it says.

# qctl openspec/specs/list-items/spec.md
Under x, x SHALL write each folded or literal item of a block list field on one line, reading as the same text.
A non-note item holding a line break SHALL stay one item, with x escapes.
A rewritten item's comments SHALL stay above it.
x SHALL fail on such an item and write nothing, and a second x SHALL change nothing.

# qctl openspec/specs/list-items/spec.md
Under x, the default, every verb that writes an item of a row's list field SHALL write it on one line, as the item's own line of the block list, and never as a folded or literal block.
The item SHALL be plain when plain text reads back as the same string, and quoted as the ledger's x say otherwise.
Under x, the item SHALL be written as yamled's automatic layout writes it: on one line, or as a literal block when its text holds a line break.

# qctl README.md
Clap remains the command grammar.
Templates under x retain skill and instruction prose while ctl-core x renders their shared version, mounted invocation/no-x rule, and visible command inventory.
Tests byte-compare both committed documents.

# qctl skills/qctl/SKILL.md
x is an ordered list; each repeated x is one item.
Use separate items for distinct facts.
Every item is one line unless the ledger declares x, so a note holding a line break is refused: pass each line as its own x.
Follow a note's date with a comma, not a colon, so it stays unquoted; do not rewrite existing notes for this.
Qctl does not mechanically wrap sentences.

# qctl skills/qctl/SKILL.md
archive — done or dropped.
Newest first.
IDs never reused.

# qctl src/instructions.md
A row names an OpenSpec change through its x path: a file under x, or under x once the change is archived.
A change mentioned only in a note is not named.
One change may serve several rows; a row names at most one.

# qctl src/instructions.md
x moves a queued row to archive with x; x records work that will not be done.
x archives every queued id a commit-body trailer closed.
Default scan is the same history x uses.
x narrows it.
x reads the hook stdin.
If it wrote the ledger, it exits non-zero so the file can be committed; it does not amend.

# qctl src/instructions.md
x is x.
x is a list of strings on every row, an archived row may carry x, and a horizon row names its x.
A schema 4 or 5 ledger still calls that field x: run x once, which renames the key in place and sets version 6.
A schema 3 ledger also has scalar notes: x splits those on blank-line paragraphs as well.
Other verbs refuse versions 3, 4, and 5 and name that command.
A ledger on an earlier version is not rewritten.

# qctl src/instructions.md
x is an ordered list.
Every repeated x is one item, so separate facts instead of building one unbounded paragraph.
Under the default x, every list item is one line: a note holding a line break is refused, so pass each line as its own x.
Under x, such a note is written as one x literal block.
Qctl never inserts mechanical line breaks mid-sentence.

# quickbar AGENTS.md
Every private symbol is looked up at launch in x, and a missing one disables quickbar with a message rather than crashing.
List each symbol in README.md "How it works".

# quickbar AGENTS.md
A menu bar app that shows the hidden menu bar of a full-screen space as soon as the pointer enters the menu bar band.
README.md describes what it does and how it works.

# rawr AGENTS.md
The pull request's CI verifies: x is the one gate.
Behaviour changes start as an OpenSpec change under x.
Releases are patch bumps, and a release is cut only when the maintainer asks for one.

# rawr openspec/changes/fullscreen-picker/design.md
A click selects, and a click on the selected row resumes.
A left click on a row that is not selected selects it; a left click on the selected row resumes it.
Two clicks on a row therefore resume it at any speed.
Alternative: a timed double click resumes, and a click on the selected row does nothing.
It lost: the maintainer chose the faster path, and a click on the highlighted row reads as "open this one".
Cost: the first row starts selected, so one click on it resumes at once.

# rawr openspec/changes/fullscreen-picker/design.md
Selection and hover are separate.
The selected row has the x pointer and a bold title in bright blue (ANSI 12); a hovered row turns its dim text to the default foreground.
Hover changes only the text colour, so it never reads as selected.
Body text uses the terminal's default foreground and secondary text is dimmed, so both read in light and dark themes.

# rawr openspec/changes/fullscreen-picker/design.md
Alternate screen on stderr.
The picker enters the alternate screen (DEC mode 1049) on stderr and leaves it on exit, so the pane's restored scrollback is shown again unchanged.
Alternative: an inline viewport under the restored output.
It lost: it pushes the output up, leaves its rows in scrollback, and cannot use the whole pane for long lists.

# rawr openspec/changes/install-and-shells/design.md
The formula lives in a tap of its own.
x holds formulae for the organization's own tools; x stays for patched forks.
The formula installs the binary and generates completions with x, and its caveats print the x line.

# rawr openspec/changes/install-and-shells/design.md
Completions come from ctl-core's x verb.
It prints a short registration script that asks the installed binary for candidates at each TAB, so completions always match the installed version.
Homebrew installs it with x, with no shell configuration.

# rawr openspec/changes/install-and-shells/design.md
x output is static.
It depends only on the shell and the rawr version, never on the machine, so it can be saved once with x and sourced with no process at shell start.
Alternative: x as the only form.
It lost as the only form: it runs rawr in every new shell, a few milliseconds that add to start time; it stays the shortest form for those who accept that.

# rawr openspec/changes/install-and-shells/design.md
Targets: macOS on Apple silicon, Linux on glibc.
x, x and x, built on free GitHub-hosted runners.
Intel Macs and musl builds are out.

# rawr openspec/changes/install-and-shells/design.md
bash, zsh and fish.
These are the shells Homebrew installs completions for, and each has a hook that runs once before the first prompt: x in bash, x in zsh, the x event in fish.
Alternative: also nushell, PowerShell and others that mise supports.
It lost for now: their quoting and x differ again, and nobody has asked for them.

# rawr openspec/changes/install-and-shells/design.md
Tools that hook into the shell split into two camps.
[mise], [zoxide] and [starship] print the line to add and leave the shell files alone; their installers end with instructions.
The [uv] installer, built by cargo-dist, appends to shell profiles unless x is set.
rawr follows the first camp.

# rawr openspec/changes/install-and-shells/proposal.md
rawr only helps once two hooks are in place: one in the shell, to offer a restored pane its sessions, and one in Claude Code, to record them.
Today both are snippets in the README, for zsh only, and there is no way to install rawr but building it.
Installing must not touch the user's shell files or settings unasked, and must not add a process to every shell start for those who keep their configuration in files.

