# rawr openspec/changes/session-search/design.md
A session's title is its first prompt.
Search rows show the first line of the first prompt that is not a slash command such as x, up to 120 characters, ending in x when cut.
The pane's own sessions keep the title from their transcript, as before.
Alternative: read the generated title from each matching transcript.
It lost: it opens transcripts on every key press, and a first prompt says what the session was for.

# rawr openspec/changes/session-search/design.md
Words match by substring, all of them, ignoring case.
A query of x matches a session whose prompts, folder or ID contain both words.
Alternative: fuzzy subsequence matching, as fzf does.
It lost: over long prompts nearly every short query matches every session, and the order stops meaning anything.

# rawr openspec/changes/session-search/specs/session-search/spec.md
THEN the picker closes, prints nothing and exits 1

# rawr README.md
Search matches every word typed, ignoring case, against the prompts typed in a session, its folder and its ID.
It reads Claude Code's prompt history, x, and lists x once to keep only sessions whose transcript is still there; it opens no transcript.
Search rows take their title from the session's first prompt that is not a slash command such as x.

# rawr README.md
Rex restores sessions, tabs and panes after a restart, but each pane comes back as a fresh shell.
x remembers which Claude Code sessions ran in each pane, and in a restored pane offers them again in a full-screen picker.
Choosing one resumes it in that pane; dismissing the picker leaves a plain shell.

# rgbctl AGENTS.md
No daemon, no boot service.
The controller keeps a saved setting through power cycles.
Effects that need a running process, such as colour by CPU temperature, are out of scope.

# rgbctl AGENTS.md
Write cycle.
Read, decode, change the owned bytes, write, read back, compare.
A read-back that differs from what was written outside the save flag is an error.

# rgbctl AGENTS.md
Config.
x (or x) names the file.
Otherwise rgbctl uses the nearest x walking up from the working directory, then x (x).
No file means built-in defaults.
The file holds named presets and the name of the default one.

# rgbctl AGENTS.md
Permissions come from a udev rule granting a group access to the hidraw node (see x).
The binary never escalates.

# rgbctl docs/hardware-test-2026-11-08.md
Byte 7 of a x reply is the port's Gen2 strip count; byte 7 of a x reply is 1 when the port is in Gen2 mode.
Byte 8 of port 0's x reply is x, which no source explains.

# rgbctl docs/hardware-test-2026-11-08.md
The second run assumed the whole cooler was on JARGB_V2_3; Test G in the fourth run established the map above.
So Tests A to C wrote to the pump head's header, not the radiator fans'.

