# unixd AGENTS.md
The service manager owns the socket.
launchd on macOS and x on Linux, one x implementation each.
No call site outside the activation module branches on the platform.
The daemon never spawns itself.

# unixd AGENTS.md
x must never depend on an async runtime.
Its consumer's direct path has no reactor.
Only the daemon coalesces; the direct path relies on the key lock.

# unixd AGENTS.md
Read x before writing code.
It is the contract, not a sketch.

# unixd AGENTS.md
Lints are denied, not warned.
x is the gate.

# unixd crates/unixd-cache/README.md
Synchronous.
No async runtime, no database.
The same cache is correct whether a daemon or a direct in-process call owns it.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
systemd fails the service after x starts (5 per 10 s by default), and the socket after x activations (20 per 2 s by default for x).
A socket failed this way refuses every connection until x.
Measured on Linux with a 1 s idle timeout and a connection every 0.9 to 1.2 s: under the defaults the service hit its start limit after 10 starts, the socket entered x, and 6 of 25 connections were refused.
With x in a 10 s interval, all 25 connections succeeded across 14 starts, including connections that arrived while the daemon was exiting.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
Neither crate renders anything.
A consumer that wants a daemon status or cache statistics to look native in its own output can depend on a semantic document layer and have these crates emit documents rather than strings.
That dependency must stay optional and must not pull a terminal renderer, a serializer, or an argument parser into either crate.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
Activation proof.
A throwaway example daemon receives its socket on stdin from launchd on macOS and from systemd on Linux, serves, exits idle, and is relaunched by the next connection.
This decides the activation path before any crate code depends on it.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
This path is chosen over the named-socket APIs for one reason.
No maintained crate hands a macOS caller an owned listener from x without the caller writing x to adopt a raw descriptor.
On Linux, x does return an owned listener, and it is the fallback if the stdin path fails its live proof.
launchd's man page asks new jobs to avoid x; the live proof in slice 1 decides whether that warning matters here.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
Neither macOS nor Linux cleans a plain program's cache directory, so the cache bounds itself.
Every limit is a x field with a default; the maintenance research compares them with other tools.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
Secrets never go into the disk cache, encrypted or not.
A daemon that needs to hold one keeps it in memory, in the secret store.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
The daemon never starts itself.
The platform service manager creates the socket, listens on it, and starts the daemon on the first connection:

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
The cache names each cached request kind in one enum and has a lookup and a store method per kind.
x takes caller-built keys and a caller policy, and stores any serializable value.

# unixd openspec/specs/activation/spec.md
WHEN the socket unit is started with x and the service is inactive

# unixd openspec/specs/cache/spec.md
When a write crosses a hard cap, the crate SHALL remove expired entries, then the least recently used, until entries and bytes are at or below their targets.
It SHALL NOT remove an unexpired entry while an expired one remains, except that it SHALL skip an entry whose key another caller holds.

# unixd openspec/specs/lifecycle/spec.md
THEN the socket file still exists and the next connection starts a new daemon

# unixd README.md
macOS on Apple Silicon and Linux.
The platform service manager owns the socket and starts the daemon on demand: launchd on macOS, x on Linux.
The daemon never spawns itself.

# verctl docs/brand.md
A 32-unit square, corner radius 6.
Line from x to x at 2 units wide.
Nodes on that line at x and x at r 3.
The new tag at x at r 4.75.
Every node centre satisfies x, so the line needs no separate alignment, and the three sit 7.5 units apart in x.
The figure clears the field by 4.5 units at the first node and 4.75 at the tag.

# verctl docs/served-files.md
Give the version a right-hand boundary.
A match ending in literal text has one, so x reads x as no match at all and the arity stops the release.
A match ending at the placeholder has none, and will rewrite the numeric head of a longer version, leaving the tail.
x is the boundary for a line the match owns; otherwise put the text that follows into x.
Nothing can be inferred for you here: x against x is correct and pins 1.2.3, so a rule that refused every trailing x would stop real releases.

# verctl openspec/changes/building-blocks/tasks.md
2026-11-09, partly shipped: x writes no lockfile outside tests.
The lockfile ownership scenario needs steps (1.2).

# verctl openspec/changes/command-renames/specs/command-names/spec.md
x SHALL rewrite each x entry, then render every template in x, at the versions on HEAD.
It SHALL take every flag the pins rewrite took in verctl 0.7.

# verctl openspec/changes/docs-build-check/specs/docs-build/spec.md
The x job SHALL run after x finishes, whether a leg failed or not, and SHALL NOT delay x or the next version commit's publish.
A failed docs.rs build SHALL NOT make a release incomplete.

# verctl openspec/changes/package-asset-files/proposal.md
A package asset tarball holds exactly one file.
x runs x (x), so a package whose release is a daemon, its client, a systemd unit, and a licence ships a tarball with only the first of the four.
Probed on verctl 0.7.2 with a x package and a x target: the tarball listed one entry.
Vendored sets already pack several files from a directory (x and x); package assets cannot.

# verctl openspec/changes/package-resolution/design.md
See proposal.md for why, and the spec for what.
Today a package's x must name a manifest file.
The stock publisher is chosen from the file name: x for x, x for x.
x is parsed and validated once, at x, and nothing downstream checks it again.
The version sources a path can resolve to are in x.

# verctl openspec/changes/registry-publish-check/design.md
It is one of the parts reusable-release-workflow was split into.
The release at a version commit is release-at-version-commit, and the docs.rs check is docs-build-check.

# verctl openspec/changes/registry-publish-check/tasks.md
1.1 x on publisher recipes, stock x and x queries, and the pre- and post-publish checks.
Proof: the idempotent-publish registry scenarios pass as tests with a fake publisher and query, including a mocked crates.io index that lacks the version of a workspace member

# verctl openspec/changes/release-state-machine/design.md
See proposal.md for why and research.md for the prior art.
Today verctl keeps no model of a release.
Each command rebuilds the state it needs from indirect signals:

# verctl openspec/changes/release-state-machine/proposal.md
Ship 0.4.0 from an integration branch x, then patches (design 4).
Contradicted: 0.4.0 to 0.7.4 shipped from x, no x exists, and the 0.8.0 minor is held on a stack instead.

# verctl openspec/changes/release-state-machine/proposal.md
All breaking changes ship in one minor, 0.8.0, held with the rest of the minor backlog; later work ships as patches that add without breaking.
0.4.0 to 0.7.4 shipped before this change, and the parts of it they carried are listed in audit.md.

# verctl openspec/changes/release-state-machine/research.md
x refreshes workspace versions from Bun 1.4.1 (oven-sh/bun#41302).
On 1.4.0 it left the lockfile stale.

# verctl openspec/changes/release-state-machine/research.md
pnpm 12 is written in Rust.
Read at v12.12.1, with its official binary run in a scratch workspace.

# verctl openspec/changes/release-state-machine/research.md
bun.lock decides published dependency versions.
It records a x for every non-root workspace package (bun.lock.rs), and x and x replace x with the version from the lockfile, not from package.json (pack_command.rs).
A run with Bun 1.4.0, 1.4.2, and 1.4.3 confirmed it: after a version bump, a pack without a lockfile refresh shipped the old dependency version.

# verctl openspec/changes/release-state-machine/research.md
Reuse.
The packing, publish, lockfile, and versioning crates are MIT but declare x (publish/Cargo.toml); none is on crates.io.

# verctl openspec/changes/semver-release-type/specs/semver-check/spec.md
x SHALL leave out a crates.io crate whose manifest has no library target as Cargo resolves it (an explicit x table or an auto-discovered x), or whose library is a proc-macro.
cargo-semver-checks does not check such a crate, and x SHALL run no check for it.
The 1.0 rule below does not apply to such a crate.

# verctl openspec/changes/version-files/design.md
See proposal.md for why, and the spec for what.
Today a package's x must name a manifest file.
The stock drivers are x (TOML keys) and x (JSON keys), chosen from the file name, and any other file needs a x entry.
The stock publisher is also chosen from the file name: x for x, x for x.
x is parsed and validated once, at x, and nothing downstream checks it again.

# verctl openspec/changes/version-pr-proposal/design.md
Each job's first verctl step reads x and fails when it is below 0.8.0.
The message names the installed version and the minimum, and says to move the verctl pin and the workflow ref together.
The minimum is a literal in the workflow, raised only when the workflow needs a newer verctl.

# verctl skills/verctl/SKILL.md
x runs x on each crates.io crate whose next release is a patch, so only an API break fails, and skips a crate with a pending x fragment or a minor the Version PR already moved.
A crate at 1.0 or later fails: the rule covers 0.x.

# yamled AGENTS.md
Tasks follow one layout.
x holds tools, settings, and deps, and its x lists x first, then the pinned x catalogs.
Every other task is a one-line fragment or a file task in x.
Fragment names are not namespaced by file, so each name carries its prefix (x in x).

# yamled AGENTS.md
Tests put YAML in x blocks.
A unit test binds the expected text before the assertion (x), so rustfmt keeps it readable.
A test of an edit sequence goes in x: its x helper reads each edited value back through x, and an inline x snapshot records the whole file with each line marked x, x, or kept.
Review a changed snapshot with x.

# yamled openspec/specs/edits/spec.md
x SHALL add a key and value to a block mapping at its end, before a named key, or after a named key, at the mapping's indentation.
x SHALL append an item to a block sequence at the sequence's indentation.
Pushing into an empty flow sequence x SHALL turn it into a block sequence under its key.
A new nested value SHALL follow the file's list style: items indented under their key, or at the key's column.

# yamled openspec/specs/edits/spec.md
A document SHALL carry one quote preference, x, set with x and x by default, and every writer, flow sequence items included, SHALL follow it.
Each preference SHALL write a string plain when it reads back unquoted; otherwise x SHALL write single quotes and x double quotes.
A character only double quotes can carry, such as a control character, SHALL get double quotes.

# yamled openspec/specs/edits/spec.md
Every edit SHALL be applied as a byte splice of the source.
Bytes outside the spliced range SHALL be identical before and after the edit.
After each edit the document SHALL parse the result; when the result is not valid YAML, the edit SHALL fail and the source SHALL stay unchanged.

# yamled openspec/specs/edits/spec.md
The writer SHALL choose a string's style from one pass over its bytes, with no parse and no serialization: whether it can be plain in a block or flow context, which quotes it takes, and whether a block scalar holds it.
The family reader, serde-saphyr with strict booleans, SHALL read every written form back as the same string, and plain SHALL be chosen exactly when that reader reads the unquoted text back as the string.

# yamled openspec/specs/edits/spec.md
An edit SHALL return x, and leave the source unchanged, for a value it cannot write in place: a key with no x after it, an empty value inside a flow collection, and an empty document.
No edit SHALL panic on any input the parser accepts.

# yamled openspec/specs/location-index/spec.md
A comment line directly above a node, with no blank line between them, SHALL belong to that node.
A comment after a node's value on the same line SHALL belong to that node.
A node's owned range SHALL start at its first owned comment line and end at the end of its last line.

# yamled README.md
x edits a YAML file the way a person would.
It replaces, removes, and inserts the values you name, and leaves comments, blank lines, quoting, block and flow style, and indentation as they were.
It is pure Rust, built on x spans and x values.

# actions AGENTS.md
A step that several jobs of one workflow run, such as the toolchain install, is a YAML anchor repeated by alias.
GitHub Actions resolves anchors but not merge keys (x), so an aliased step is identical everywhere.
A job adjusts it only through the environment it sets.

# actions AGENTS.md
Shared workflows take concrete inputs, with no visibility detection or abstract runner modes.
What depends on the event, and cache-write safety, stay decisions at run time.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
The job restores the mise install cache and mbx's objects from the GitHub Actions cache instead.
x saves under its own key suffix.
A push to the default branch always builds, so the mbx cache saved from it stays current.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
The caller selects a persistent cache volume with x.
It must then supply its read-only safety label and a positive x that matches its runner labels.
The render fragment derives the size label and the trim limit from one value, and render tests check they match.
The workflow refuses a run that lacks this configuration.

# actions docs/rust-ci.md
A cache step that fails does not fail the job, since the result comes from the build and tests.
One example is a save refused because the commit's key is already reserved on a re-run.
Only the build job writes the build cache.

# actions policy/README.md
A workflow on x groups runs by x.
The group may end with x, so a draft run never cancels the ready one.
A workflow that also runs on x may end its group with the queue expression, which gives every merge queue entry one group per base branch, so a newer entry cancels the runs below it.
The queue merges on the head entry's result alone, and that commit holds every entry below it.
Outside the queue the expression gives the pull request group.
It cancels the run a new push replaces, always or for pull request runs only.

# actions policy/README.md
the hosted and other accepted runner labels;

# ctl-core ctl-core/AGENTS.md
The baselines are plain files, not insta snapshots, because the 0.6.x files stay frozen once committed.
The render-engine change adds the new engine's outputs beside them and lists each difference in its pull request.

# ctl-core ctl-core/README.md
x is a tree of semantic blocks: headings, prose, fields, tables, sections, notices, rules, and verbatim text.
It holds meaning, not escape codes or table borders.
The renderer chooses style, wrapping, width, and colour, through the x, x, and x crates.

# ctl-core ctl-layout/AGENTS.md
The layout rules of the widgets.
They cover how a table chooses column widths and how a record and a rule fill a width.
They also cover how a stack and an indent compose children, and how a breakpoint picks the widget for a width.

# ctl-core docs/presentation.md
An automatically detected TTY or x width reserves two columns before wrapping, stacking, help, fields, and table layout.
This default protects hosts that indent tool output or whose frame consumes the rightmost column.
The configured floor is 20 when the detected width permits it.
A narrower terminal caps that floor, so ctl-core never claims columns the terminal does not have.

# ctl-core docs/presentation.md
Wrapping breaks at whitespace.
A word wider than its column, such as a URL or a path, is not split in records, paragraphs, and headings.
It runs past the width.
That keeps it copyable whole.
Grid tables still split it, to keep their borders intact.

# ctl-core openspec/specs/env-flags/spec.md
Bind every long option of a ctl CLI to an environment variable under one prefix.
A caller that holds its settings in the environment can then run the bare verb.
A flag wins over its variable, the variable over the config file, and the config file over the default.

# ctl-core openspec/specs/features/spec.md
All 12 directional formatting characters of UAX #9 at Unicode 16.0.0.
They are ALM, LRM, RLM, LRE, RLE, PDF, LRO, RLO, LRI, RLI, FSI, and PDI.

# ctl-core openspec/specs/usage-spec/spec.md
For every command in the committed corpus, x SHALL return the bytes usage-lib 7.3.0 returns for the same command and bin name.
The values the next requirement covers are the only exception.

# exa-cli AGENTS.md
API-key precedence is x → x → stored credential.
x manages macOS Keychain, Linux Secret Service, or Windows Credential Manager through the x addon under one service/account pair (x / x).
The Linux addon links no x, so no x is needed, but a D-Bus Secret Service provider must be running.
Secure-storage failure is fatal unless login explicitly receives x; that fallback is an atomic x plaintext file.
Tests inject x and never touch a real store.

# fnox-export README.md
Daemon-cached by default.
Forces fnox's per-user daemon on (x).
Once the daemon is warmed for a profile, resolutions that miss mise's env cache — a new shell, an expired TTL, an offsite x — are served from the daemon's memory (~0.1s) instead of re-hitting the provider (seconds each).
The first warm-up still resolves live.
Opt out with x.

# forkctl docs/brand.md
All the ink parts are subpaths of one x, and the same for rust.
As separate xs they each antialias against the field, so a shared edge on a fractional coordinate composites to roughly 75% coverage and shows as a grey hairline through the figure — measured at the bus edge, x where it should be x.
One path is rasterised in one pass and the seam cannot occur.
Anything added to this mark goes in the same path.

# forkctl examples/README.md
x shows the complete tracked shape after source and tooling patches exist.
Source exports are deterministic under x; do not declare per-patch export paths.
Persistent ownership uses x globs where x stays within a path segment and x crosses directories.

# forkctl openspec/specs/cli-environment/spec.md
forkctl SHALL read each long option, global options included, from x when the command line does not give it, where x is the option's long name in upper case with x replaced by x.
A flag on the command line SHALL win over its variable, and an empty variable SHALL count as unset.
Help, version, hidden options, and the x side of a pair SHALL NOT read a variable.

# forkctl openspec/specs/manifest-upgrade/spec.md
x SHALL fail with x and write nothing while an operation journal exists, because that journal belongs to the forkctl that started it.
It SHALL fail with x and write nothing for a manifest whose schema is neither 1 nor 2.
On a schema 2 manifest with a clean worktree it SHALL succeed and change nothing.

# forkctl README.md
Most long options have collision-audited mnemonic shorts.
Leaf parameters are grouped by subject, metadata/scope, capture, execution, and output.
Help is generated from Clap metadata through ctl-core; no second parameter specification exists.
Pretty help, result tables, notices, and errors wrap to the detected terminal width; captured non-TTY output may provide the standard x fallback.
JSON, schemas, Usage KDL, and completion scripts never reflow.

# forkctl README.md
Persistent ownership uses x globs (x stays within a segment; x crosses directories).
One-shot capture uses Git pathspecs.
Forkctl never guesses intent.

# forkctl README.md
The API already accepts complete typed command arguments from JSON on stdin; x selects either a YAML or JSON manifest path.
This is the generic programmatic interface — no second stdin argument grammar exists.

# forkctl skills/forkctl/SKILL.md
If a hook leaves files dirty, refresh and edit stop in phase x: stage the paths the patch owns (such as x after a pin change), restore the others, and run x.
Remove, disable, enable, and rebase stop in x: restore those paths and x.
x refuses while an operation is in flight.

# hakkiri README.md
Planned, not usable yet.
The first release lints what Vale lints today with ProseStyle 0.0.7.
It adds rules that count a habit per sentence or per number of words.
An editor server follows in the second release.

# herdr-restore-notice AGENTS.md
A standalone Herdr plugin.
Read README and coding standards.

# opencode-cursor-inference docs/decisions.md
Decision: This supersedes the preceding V1-primary decision.
OpenCode V2 beta x is the design center, and stable OpenCode x is a compatibility target.
Publish one hybrid default module whose V2 x and stable-V1 x members share auth, catalog, provider, and lifecycle primitives.
Export that module at both the package root and x; keep x as the AI SDK x entry.

# opencode-cursor-inference README.md
The package sends the complete provider prompt, declared tool schemas, tool results, and supported image inputs to Cursor for inference.
OpenCode executes tool calls locally according to its permission configuration.
Treat both your OpenCode permissions and Cursor account as security boundaries.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
The tagline gap is exactly half the chip gap, and that ratio is load bearing.
At the card's 0.19333× those three gaps become 10.1, 4.3 and 8.5 px.
Equalise them and the card shows three equidistant grey lines instead of a wordmark with its subtitle and a separate chip.

# pi-cursor docs/brand.md
No two slabs touch.
The magenta slab therefore abuts nothing, and no ink-to-accent antialias seam is possible anywhere in the file.
The three graphite slabs are subpaths of one x for the same reason: as separate xs each antialiases against the field, so a shared edge on a fractional coordinate composites to roughly 75% coverage and shows as a grey hairline through the figure.
One path rasterises in one pass.
Anything added to this mark goes in the same path.

# pi-cursor docs/decisions.md
2026-09-03 A fresh OAuth live proof passed all three catalog calls, DashboardService usage, and a bounded Composer 2.5 RunInference response.
A visible isolated Pi TUI in Herdr rendered live Enterprise Summary and Models views, switched tabs, closed normally, and exposed one timed-out aggregate sample as a named partial miss.
The temporary access token and pane were removed after proof.
This establishes the current Darwin account path, not Linux/Windows host behavior or future server compatibility.

# pi-cursor docs/protocol/turn-accounting-source-audit-2026-09-04.md
The formatted client proves that x is a preflight display, not measured usage.
It contains no conversation or invocation identifier.
x is present in generated schema and service descriptors but has no caller anywhere in the packaged 3.18.9 desktop workbench or agent-host JavaScript.
Its name and wire fields are evidence of a conversation/time-bounded cost query; without a client caller or server implementation, they do not establish its timestamp unit, settlement timing, returned item meanings, or whether concurrent requests can be separated.

# pi-cursor docs/protocol/turn-accounting-source-audit-2026-09-04.md
The event schema has no x, request ID, generation ID, or response ID.
The response's raw x has price data but no invocation field.
Full-workbench symbol search finds only the generated filtered-method descriptor; the packaged desktop client does not call it.
Cursor IDE 3.18.25 retains this field set and absence of a caller.

# pi-cursor docs/protocol/turn-accounting-source-audit-2026-09-04.md
No typed RunInference response message has a price, cents, charge, billing, or cost field.
The x arm can carry arbitrary keys, so the schema alone cannot prove that a future server will never place cost there.
The formatted client treats it as an opaque object and contains no logic that reads a billed-cost key from it.
Module x maps the usage and extended-usage arms directly to their token values, resolves the invocation ID separately, and does not calculate or recover money from the stream.

# pi-cursor README.md
Persist diagnostics defaults to off.
When enabled, each assistant message carries a x diagnostic in session JSONL.
Its reconciliation census is payload-free; the same diagnostic can also retain provider metadata, image descriptions, and inference-extra side-channel data.
The current structural census is about 0.6 kB per assistant message (roughly 56 kB per 100 turns); provider side channels can make it larger.
When disabled, Cursor adds no assistant-message diagnostic data.

# pi-cursor README.md
The pane uses captured DashboardService calls and renderer-defined units.
It does not infer prices from model tokens or substitute values for missing samples.
Optional failed calls remain visible as named misses while available usage still renders.
Outside TUI mode, print writes plain text, JSON emits a custom message event, and RPC uses a notification.

# prose-style AGENTS.md
Each rule catches one habit.
Its comment states why, in one or two short sentences.
No chat quotes, no profanity, no private paths or tool names.

# prose-style AGENTS.md
Match the habit.
Let natural uses of the same word pass with x.
Check each hit on a real document before raising a level.

# prose-style openspec/changes/frequency-habit-rules/research.md
Agent docs use semicolons seven times as often as design docs, and colons inside a sentence four times as often.
Agents write ", not the" contrasts seven times as often, and almost never the human "not X but Y".
Humans write "we" and ask questions far more.
Agent replies average 13.4 words per sentence with a standard deviation of 5.8; in human text the deviation runs from 9 to 11.

# qctl openspec/changes/horizon-reason/proposal.md
x upgrades a schema 5 ledger to 6 by renaming each horizon row's x key to x where it is written, keeping its value, quotes, and comments.
It upgrades schema 3 and 4 ledgers the same way, after the steps it already takes for them.

# qctl openspec/changes/horizon-reason/tasks.md
2.2 Move every test ledger to version 6 and x, and accept the changed mutation snapshots from a test run with x.
Verify the mutation snapshots pass in CI.

# qctl openspec/specs/ledger-style/spec.md
x SHALL rewrite each string scalar written on one line whose quoting differs from what the declared x write, keeping its value.
It SHALL leave block scalars, keys, values that are not strings, and the x block as written.
x SHALL fail on such a scalar and write nothing.

# qctl openspec/specs/list-items/spec.md
Under x, x and x SHALL refuse a x value that holds a line break, before they write anything.
The error SHALL say that a note is one line and that each line is passed as its own x.
Under x, such a note SHALL be written as one literal block.

# qctl README.md
Acceptance states observable, demonstrably true end conditions for closing the task.
Write the resulting state in the present tense.
Put implementation steps in x and supporting context in x.
Repeat x for distinct facts; each argument is one ordered list item, written on one line unless the ledger declares x.

# qctl skills/qctl/SKILL.md
Treat x as the policy owner for x.
Do not reimplement validation with a copied Ajv test.
Do not vendor x.

# qctl src/instructions.md
x includes the complete ledger state.
The JSON from x carries x as an array and exits non-zero when it is not empty.

# rawr openspec/changes/fullscreen-picker/design.md
A title comes from the transcript's last megabyte.
Transcripts reach hundreds of megabytes, and Claude Code writes the title rows again every few dozen kilobytes, the user's title next to each generated one.
Reading the last 1 MiB finds the current title without reading the rest.
Alternative: read the whole transcript.
It lost: the picker runs as a shell starts, and local transcripts measured up to 417 MB, each read whole at every start.

# rawr openspec/changes/install-and-shells/specs/install/spec.md
THEN zsh completes x with no change to the user's shell files

# rawr openspec/changes/session-search/proposal.md
x in the picker opens a search line.
Each key press filters every resumable local Claude Code session by the words typed, matched against the prompts the user typed in it, its folder and its ID.

# rgbctl docs/protocol.md
The per-zone records the board holds by default are mode x with rainbow colours at slow speed (x).
x without colours writes the same mode and colour source to the last record at its default medium speed (style x).
Observed: from a clean state that write gave a smooth moving rainbow on every device, the cooler included.

# rgbctl docs/protocol.md
JARGB_V2_1: two Cooler Master MF120 Halo fans, chained.
Each has 24 LEDs, and units made after 1 June 2021 are Addressable Gen 2 RGB (Cooler Master specification).
OpenRGB's sizing table lists the Halo as 24 LEDs wired in parallel (OpenRGB resize guide); whether the chained second fan repeats the first's data or continues it is not established.

# rgbctl openspec/specs/lighting/spec.md
rgbctl SHALL read presets from x or x, else the nearest x above the working directory, else x.
It SHALL refuse an unknown field, a wave of more than four colours, and a x that names no preset.

# unixd AGENTS.md
Verification is named per change.
A behaviour claim carries the check that proves it.
The design document's verification table is the baseline, and step 7 is a required regression.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
Synchronous, no reactor, no database.
Versioned validated JSON envelopes on disk; directories x, files x.
No existing crate covers stale-if-error, so the policy is this crate's own code; the pieces under it come from x and one small crate.

# unixd docs/design/daemon-and-cache.md
Every accepted connection is checked for the same effective UID as the serving process, with x, before any frame is read.
It uses x on macOS and x on Linux.
A mismatch closes the connection without a reply and without a log entry containing the peer's identity.
The client checks the listener the same way, and also accepts root on macOS, because launchd creates every agent's socket as root; there the x directory around the socket keeps other users out.

# unixd README.md
Both crates are implemented and unreleased (x).
x has the transport, serving from the service manager's socket with drain and idle exit, unit install for launchd and systemd, single flight, and an in-memory secret store.
Read x for the contract, and x for the evidence behind it, before changing code.

# yamled AGENTS.md
Format-preserving YAML edits for Rust.
A library without a command.
Import as x.

# yamled openspec/specs/location-index/spec.md
x SHALL index one YAML document so that every scalar, sequence, and mapping node can be found by a x of mapping keys and sequence indexes.
The index SHALL record each node's value byte range, its key's byte range when it is a mapping value, its style, and the number of children of a collection.
A source that is not valid YAML SHALL be refused with the parser's line and column.
